# Line-ending policy. Added 2026-07-28 (full-repo audit).
#
# Why: the repo had none, so normalization depended entirely on each machine's
# `core.autocrlf`. It drifted — a docs commit stored CRLF where its parent had
# stored LF, and git rendered a two-line edit as a 971-line rewrite. Churn like
# that does not corrupt anything, but it destroys the reviewability of history,
# which for a project whose registers ARE the deliverable is a real cost.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf`: git decides text-vs-binary, normalizes every text file to
# LF in the repository, and checks out LF in the working tree. Windows tools
# here (PowerShell, Python, .NET, Unity) all read LF fine, and pinning eol
# makes the result identical on every machine instead of per-config.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary — never touch these bytes, never try to diff them as text.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.gif    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.dll    binary
*.so     binary
*.dylib  binary
*.zip    binary

# PowerShell hooks are invoked by powershell.exe, which handles LF fine.
# Left under the global rule deliberately — no exception needed.

# Unity asset/meta files are text (YAML) and must normalize with everything
# else, or Unity rewrites them on import and every clone diverges.
*.meta          text eol=lf
*.asset         text eol=lf
*.unity         text eol=lf
*.prefab        text eol=lf
*.inputactions  text eol=lf
